{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,2),(-1,2),(-1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-1)$\n- $(0,-2)$\n- $(-2,-1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-8427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-2,0),(-2,-1),(0,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-8427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d1c67a9430405ef72e259fb94771ab456f6453342f406192d9313ada08eb882"} {"question" "q-8427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-8427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-8427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-160619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/f3e31d1c15a97adea32790eafe75ad1bc9f70c854b516b98de6648ec8d7bcb87.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-160619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/9fae3ec0ada3008937d88e4e76374a56da4369b1821cfee072c90d88b562df46.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ae800b271848948b5f60f5cd7c1cecd163c2aa7399e9a87e2a52d158857c6a42.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-160619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d1c67a9430405ef72e259fb94771ab456f6453342f406192d9313ada08eb882"} {"question" "q-160619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-160619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/2da0ffb873eb230718aab29c7dd09e015a0801b7ecb3b7dea91102f30b3e8816.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/489bc530fc35e2e740038264f2be05bffb90214249ec5602591623d457325db3.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/5bd4d8cc2643767b82acc0e61d168f5b387be67a7ff96634604e0d6828cc288b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ba328088ca187ea28b241a302a486da1145a51c7e134d8d4f529f5f4a916ddc1.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/efb79fc1071f79597c81a0a974e7f6b21d361ac046f728625e2fe1c02bb507eb.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-160619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ba328088ca187ea28b241a302a486da1145a51c7e134d8d4f529f5f4a916ddc1.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-160621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/b75a7519c7953b7a3c5bd5b5e61e961ca26c3f0dc9e1aab32bf2c639dd95325f.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-160621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/8ea257c65769c4d6f67f55ce6533c1bfdfe45debcf1ba532b9ec3f717238507a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "9ecaac9036eda19805b5ca050c0cea5bee687d2594143180c154b2b019e394f0"} {"question" "q-160621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d1c67a9430405ef72e259fb94771ab456f6453342f406192d9313ada08eb882"} {"question" "q-160621" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_A